SUMMARY:Opening Reception for the Gran Camino (Qhapaq Ñan) Exhibition: Presented by The Consulate General of Peru
DESCRIPTION:Join us in the Auditorium for a reception for the Gran Camino 
 (Qhapaq Ñan) Exhibit -  which will be on display in the 1st floor July 
 5-July 30.\n\nWith remarks from Gabriela Porras Alor\, Consulate General of 
 Peru.\n\nAbout the Qhapaq Ñan\nThe Qhapaq Ñan is one of the most 
 important human creations of the Andean world. The\nformalization of this 
 vast road system\, designed to make use of the Andean landscape and 
 its\nresources\, not only connected communities across different regions\, 
 but also fostered an\ninteractive dynamic of social\, economic\, cultural\, 
 technological\, and ideological values. From its\ninception\, it functioned 
 as a backbone that integrated the peoples of the Tawantinsuyu\, 
 the\nresources of the diverse regions\, and the provincial administrative 
 centers\, all monitored from\nCusco.\n\n\nThe Qhapaq Ñan\, or Inca Road\, 
 is a unique architectural space that links the rich 
 historical\,\ncultural\, human\, and geographical diversity of Peru across 
 both time and territory. Since the\ntime of the Incas and continuing to the 
 present day\, this road has facilitated the movement of\npeople and become 
 the primary route of communication and ritual exchange that unites 
 the\npeoples of the Andean world.\n\n\nMore than 500 years after its 
 construction\, certain stretches of the road are still used by\nresidents 
 of nearby villages to travel from one place to another in order to carry 
 out activities\nrelated to resource production and trade\, to attend school 
 or work\, and to participate in\ntraditional festivals and 
 celebrations—thus preserving a living tradition along the route.\nToday\, 
 the Qhapaq Ñan remains a vital part of many communities that continue to 
 use it for\ncommunication and to safeguard ancestral knowledge through 
 intergenerational transmission.\nFor this reason\, Peru—through the 
 Ministry of Culture and the Qhapaq Ñan Project – 
 National\nHeadquarters—works to study\, preserve\, value\, and keep the 
 shared history of this Andean\nheritage alive.
LOCATION:Montclair Main - Auditorium
ORGANIZER;CN="Montclair Library":MAILTO:events@montclairlibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Lectures / History & Culture
UID:LibCal-10306-16047833
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/16047833
END:VEVENT
